1 SHOW

   Displays the state of DCL, the console, the processes, or the
   machine.

   Format

     SHOW keyword

   The machine's registers, memory, breakpoints, and the call stack are
   the debugger's: type DEBUG, then HELP SHOW.

2 DEBUG

   Shows govax's debugging switches, each on (its name) or off (NO and
   its name), with what it does.  SET DEBUG sets them.

   Format

     SHOW DEBUG

2 DEFAULT

   Shows the default device and directory, as SET DEFAULT set them.
   When the default device is a search list, each place it stands for
   follows on a line beginning with "=".

   Format

     SHOW DEFAULT

2 DEVICES

   Lists the devices: the terminal, the null device, mailboxes, and each
   disk, with a mounted disk's volume label, free blocks, open files, and
   mount count.

   Format

     SHOW DEVICES [name]

3 Parameter

 name

   A device's name, or the start of one: SHOW DEVICES DU shows every
   device whose name begins with DU (DUA0, DUA1, ...).  If no device's
   does, the command says %SYSTEM-W-NOSUCHDEV.  Without a name, every
   device is shown.

3 Qualifiers
/FULL

   Shows all of each device's details.  For a disk with a volume
   mounted, "Operations completed" is the number of block reads and
   writes done on the volume since it was mounted, and the size, free
   space, label, and file count are read from the volume itself.

2 EDITOR

   Shows the host editor EDIT runs, and the directory it works in, as
   SET EDITOR set them.

   Format

     SHOW EDITOR

2 LOGICAL

   Shows logical names and their equivalence strings, as VMS does.

   Format

     SHOW LOGICAL [logical-name[,...]]

3 Parameter

 logical-name[,...]

   The names to show.  Without one, every name in each table searched is
   listed under the table's name.  A name with the * or % wildcards
   lists the matching names the same way.  Any other name is shown from
   the first table that has it, followed by the translation of each
   equivalence string that is itself a logical name, numbered by level:

     $ SHOW LOGICAL MYDISK
       "MYDISK" = "WORK4" (LNM$PROCESS_TABLE)
     1 "WORK4" = "$255$DUA17:" (LNM$SYSTEM_TABLE)

3 Qualifiers
/FULL

   Also shows each name's access mode, such as [super] or [exec], and
   each equivalence string's translation attributes ([concealed],
   [terminal]).

/GROUP

   Searches only the group table.

/JOB

   Searches only the job table.

/PROCESS

   Searches only the process table.

/STRUCTURE

   Shows the logical name directories and the tables in each.

/SYSTEM

   Searches only the system table.

/TABLE

      /TABLE=(table[,...])

   Searches only the tables named.  By default the tables LNM$DCL_LOGICAL
   names are searched: the process, job, group, and system tables.

2 MEMORY

   Shows the machine's physical memory and each address region.

   Format

     SHOW MEMORY

   With paging on (VMINIT/PAGING), it also shows how many page frames
   are on the free list, on the modified list, and in use, by the kind of
   page each holds (a process's, a global section's, or the system's);
   the page faults by kind; the pages the modified page writer has
   written and the writes it took; and the page file: its name, and once
   it's open its size, its free and used blocks, and whether it has
   passed the PAGEFRAG (65%) or PAGECRIT (90%) threshold or is full.
   "Page file committed" is the pages charged against every job's
   PGFLQUOTA: a page is charged when it's made, and takes a page file
   block only when it's written there.  The page file is installed at
   startup (opened when it's first needed with vax.sysgen.paging.defer),
   so it may show as "not opened yet".

   With paging on, each region's lines describe the current process's
   address space as it is: P0's and P1's size is the pages the region
   has now, "Table frames" how many pages of its page tables hold their
   entries (only those take memory), and its virtual addresses the range
   those pages cover; S0's "Mapped pages" are those with memory behind
   them.  With paging off, each region shows the physical addresses
   VMINIT laid it out at.

3 Qualifiers
/FILES

   Shows only the paging files, as VMS does on a VAX: each one's name (a
   volume file by its volume's DISK$label logical name) and its free,
   reservable, and total pages.  Reservable pages are those not
   committed to a job's PGFLQUOTA.  govax has one page file and no swap
   file, and lists the page file only while it's open.

/FULL

   With /FILES, shows each file's details instead: its free,
   reservable, and total blocks, its paging file number (3, as on VMS),
   and how many processes use it.

/RUNTIME

   Accepted, and changes nothing.

/STATISTICS

   Accepted, and changes nothing.

2 NVRAM

   Shows the NVRAM image LOAD/NVRAM loaded: its file and its addresses
   in physical memory, or "No NVRAM initialized".

   Format

     SHOW NVRAM

2 PROCESS

   Shows a process's details: its user, process ID and name, terminal,
   user identification code (UIC), base priority, and default device and
   directory.  With no name or /IDENTIFICATION, it shows the current
   process.

   Format

     SHOW PROCESS [process-name]

3 Parameter

 process-name

   The process's name, as SHOW SYSTEM lists it (quote a name with blanks
   or lowercase).

3 Qualifiers
/IDENTIFICATION

      /IDENTIFICATION=pid

   Names the process by its process ID, in hexadecimal, as SHOW SYSTEM
   lists it.

2 RADIX

   Shows the console's radix, as a number (10, 16, or 8), which SET RADIX
   sets.

   Format

     SHOW RADIX

2 ROM

   Shows the console ROM LOAD/ROM loaded: its file and its addresses in
   physical memory, or "No ROM loaded".

   Format

     SHOW ROM

2 SHARE_PREFIX

   Shows the console's shareable image prefix, kept from the C emulator
   govax grew from.  Nothing in govax sets it, so it says "No sharable
   image prefix defined"; RUN finds shareable images as RUN
   Shareable_images describes.

   Format

     SHOW SHARE_PREFIX

2 STRING_POOL

   Shows the console's string pool: its base, size, and the next free
   place.  The pool is the S0 memory where the interactive assembler
   (ASM) keeps the string constants its statements use: a quoted string
   where an address belongs becomes a descriptor in the pool, and its
   address is the operand's value.  CLEAR STRINGS empties it.

   Format

     SHOW STRING_POOL

2 SYMBOL

   Shows DCL symbols, as DCL's SHOW SYMBOL does.  A global symbol is
   shown with "==" and a local one with "="; an "*" marks the shortest
   abbreviation of the name; an integer is shown in decimal,
   hexadecimal, and octal.  The machine's symbol table (ASM's symbols,
   and the predefined ones) is the debugger's SHOW SYMBOL.

   Format

     SHOW SYMBOL [symbol-name]

3 Parameter

 symbol-name

   The symbol to show, or an abbreviation it allows.  It is looked up in
   the local symbol table, then the global one, and the first found is
   shown.  With the wildcards * and %, every symbol whose name matches
   is shown, local symbols first.

3 Qualifiers
/ALL

   Shows every symbol in the table: the local one, unless /GLOBAL is
   given.

/GLOBAL

   Searches only the global symbol table.

/LOCAL

   Searches only the current command level's local symbol table.

3 Examples

   1.$ SHOW SYMBOL *
       FO*RTH == "$DUA1:[TOOLS]FORTH.EXE"
       N = 42   Hex = 0000002A  Octal = 00000000052

     Shows every symbol.

2 SYSTEM

   Lists the processes, as VMS's DCL does: each one's process ID, name,
   scheduling state, current priority, I/O, CPU time, page faults, and
   pages.  The first line names the system as govax and its version, and
   the node as the host computer's name.  States are VMS's: CUR is the
   process the CPU is running, COM one ready to run, and the wait states
   are HIB (hibernating), LEF and CEF (waiting for a local or common
   event flag), SUSP, and a resource wait such as RWMBX (room in a full
   mailbox).  I/O is the process's buffered and direct I/O operations,
   and Pages the pages in its working set (with paging off, the pages
   its address space has in memory).

   Format

     SHOW SYSTEM

2 TRANSLATION

   Shows one level of translation of a logical name: the first match
   found, with no further translation.

   Format

     SHOW TRANSLATION logical-name

3 Qualifiers
/TABLE

      /TABLE=table

   The table to search, or a logical name that translates to a list of
   tables.  The default is LNM$DCL_LOGICAL (the process, job, group, and
   system tables).

2 VERSION

   Shows the console microkernel's version.

   Format

     SHOW VERSION

2 WORKING_SET

   Shows the current process's working set values, in pages: the
   default working set (shown as the limit), the quota, and the extent;
   whether automatic adjustment is enabled; and the authorized quota and
   extent, which SET WORKING_SET can't go beyond.

   Format

     SHOW WORKING_SET
